WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
I am under the English Channel on a train

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.680
in one of the wonders of the modern world:

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:05.660
the Channel Tunnel.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.900
And it's easy to take the tunnel for granted these days:

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:11.640
you just buy a ticket, take your somewhat ridiculous leopard print suitcase--

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:12.420
it's not mine--

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.920
on board the train, and a couple of hours later you're in Paris.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.120
And it's easy to forget just what a wonder this thing is,

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.780
and it's easy to forget that it was 200 years, nearly,

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:25.720
from the first proposal to put a train under the English Channel--

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.240
actually, it wasn't a train back then, it would've been horse drawn carrages

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.640
lit by oil lamps--

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.720
to this wonderful construction under the seabed itself.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:34.700
And here is something you might not know:

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:38.660
those older schemes, those somewhat ridiculous Victorian schemes,

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.400
some of them actually started.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:46.080
In 1881 exploratory tunnels were made about a mile out from the UK, and out from France.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.740
There was a tunnel started,

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.600
but then the British press and politicians got cold feet,

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.020
and thought it might compromise our national  security.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:57.940
And in the 1970s the British government at the time was all for building a tunnel,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.100
but then they got cold feet when the economy tanked.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:02.260
But just imagine:

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.320
we might have had a Channel Tunnel a century earlier

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:08.760
if it hadn't been for Britain being worried about its sovereignty,

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.120
although given there was World War II and Hitler really could have invaded through it,

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.420
perhaps they were onto the right idea.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.280
That is something you might not have known.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:21.160
[Closed captions by MM.
